        <section id="version-control-and-source-code-control"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Version control and Source code control</h2>
              <p>Version control is an aspect of Source control (which we looked at in Unit 3), and the terms tend to be used interchangeable.  Source control systems manage the source code versions.  Each time a product is deployed it would be identified by its version.</p>
<p>See below to learn more about the differences:</p>

              
              
  <div class="toggle-group">
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Source code control
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Source code control is a software system that helps developers to collaborate on code and keep track of changes. This is useful and important in a team of developers working on the same software project. Source code control would allow developers to work on their own section of code on their own 'feature branch', which would mean that they could work independently, and 'commit' their own work (along with other team members) to the main 'Dev branch' without any conflicts.</p>
<p>It should be noted that <em>source code control is specific to source code</em>.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s1_p2_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    <h3>Version control</h3>
    <p>Version control includes version control software, version control systems, or version control tools. <strong>Version control also covers large binary files and digital assets.</strong></p>
<p>Version control allows teams to manage changes over time.  Revisions to the project assets such as source code, graphics, videos, etc can be tracked using <strong>version control systems</strong>.  Version control is used in both hardware and software product development projects. Version control helps teams to develop products in a more efficient way and helps to deploy a product quicker.</p>
<p><img src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s3_p2_accordion2.svg" alt=""></p>

</toggle-section>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="version-control-within-a-source-code-control-system" >
          
            <h2>Version control within a Source Code control system</h2>
              <p>So going back to the source code control branch flow diagram (from Unit 3) – you will note that each time the product is deployed to the main branch it is assigned a version number.</p>
<p>A version number would be assigned each time a version is deployed - for example a piece of software would be pre-production versions as it is being developed such as v0.1, v0.2 etc, and then once the first release is deployed into the production (live) environment this would then be incremented to v1.0.  Subsequent minor updates would be named v1.1, v1.2 etc.  The next major update would be named v2.0.</p>
<p><img src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u3_s1_p3_image1.svg" alt=""></p>
<p>Once on the 'Master branch' it would have a final review and test.  It is at this stage that the stakeholders of the project would sign off that <strong>version</strong>, and then it would be deployed to the 'live site'.  <strong>It is at this stage of the project that the 'version' of the software, application or website is recorded.</strong></p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="types-of-version-control"  class="t-bg-supporting3">
          
            <h2>Types of version control</h2>
              <p>Although in most software development projects there are two types of version control systems - centralised and distributed, there is also a another 'local' type which can is used so this has been included. Scroll through the slides below to learn more about the different types of version control systems. See below to see the three different types.</p>

              
              
  <div class="toggle-group">
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Local Version Control System (LVCS)
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>This is the simplist type and is when the project files are kept on a local computer.  A local version control system (LVCS) has a simple database which keeps tracks of changes to files using a Revision Control System (RCS). So for each working file (or document) you would instate its RCS file once, and enter a cycle of checkout, change, and checkin tasks.   RCS is often compared to a library system – so for example you would accept a library card (instate), take the book home (checkout), read it, and then return it to the library (checkin).</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s3_p2_accordion3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Centralised Version Control System (CVCS)
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Issues can be encountered when development teams needed to collaboration on other systems that were not local.  To overcome this issue Centralised Version Control Systems (CVCS) were created. The history of changes to the source code, files and documents are kept on a central server.  Everyone in the team would request the latest version of the project to push their most recent changes.  All of the team would share everyone’s work.  This type of system would allow more access and use folder permissions.  They are also backed up on a regular basis and have strong security.  However, there can be issues if the server goes down or if the centralised database gets corrupted.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s3_p2_accordion4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
  
<toggle-section open="false">
    
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Distributed Version Control System (DVCS)
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>With Distributed version control systems (DVCS) the developers would check out the latest versions of the files and mirror the repository. Every checkout is a full backup of all the data as it would also be on each of the team developers local environment. Developers can have their own workflow (and their own branch) in this type of system - this isn’t possible with CVCS. The advantages of this system is that the history is fully available, it is fast because of its local way of working, it doesn’t require access to remote servers, and branching and merging is very straightforward.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s3_p2_accordion5.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
</toggle-section>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="benefits-of-version-control"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Benefits of version control</h2>
              <p>Scroll through the slides below to learn about the benefits and capabilities of version control.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Concurrent development
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Multiple developers and other team members such as designers can work on the same set of files at the same time without the concern of duplicating work or accidently overwriting other team members work.  Given the degree of complexity within a modern software product there is a need to manage multiple versions of source code files and completed products.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s3_p2_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Automation
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Automation improves productivity and the quality of a project.  It is now common in modern version control systems for Testing and Deployment to be automated.  Within the DevOps stages Continuous integration with automated builds and code reviews are standard aspects.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s3_p2_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Team collaboration
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>In the virtual world of remote working Team members may be located in different geographical areas. So there needs to be a way for all team members to have access to the same product development environment system.  A single source of truth which has secured identify and access management, and the ability to reuse IPs and software will enable the team to use the system collaborate.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s3_p2_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Preserving history
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Changes that are being made to the system are important and should include what, who, why, and when they are being made. Version control will capture detailed information on each change made and give you access to that history forever.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s3_p2_slide4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Availability and recovery
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Because you can have different versions of the source control working at the same time – this gives different members of a large team the ability to work on the same code. In cases of more complex items or server access issues - this will increase productivity and efficiency.  With some version control software you can also have a replica of the repository on a different location. Also, because everything is backed up on the system, a version could be rolled back to a previous version if there were any issues.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s3_p2_slide5.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
